<section class="almanac-section mental-model-section">
  <div class="shell mental-model-grid">
    <div class="mental-model-copy">
      <p class="almanac-kicker">The 90-second model</p>
      <h2>Six ideas prevent most category errors.</h2>
      <p>“Wasm”, “component”, “WIT”, “linker”, and “async” describe different layers. Keeping them separate makes performance and authority discussions much easier.</p>
    </div>
    <ol class="mental-model-list">
      <li><span>01</span><div><strong>WIT is a contract, not an implementation.</strong><p>It names typed imports and exports. Bindgen turns that contract into Rust glue on each side.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>02</span><div><strong>The host calls exports; the guest calls imports.</strong><p>Wasmtime’s linker supplies imports. Dekopon’s current component asks for none, so an empty linker is enough.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>03</span><div><strong>A Store is an execution context, not a thread.</strong><p>Today the calling main thread enters a fresh Store. A helper thread only watches the wall-clock deadline.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>04</span><div><strong>The model never runs inside Wasm.</strong><p>The Rust prompt loop performs HTTP, parses untrusted tool arguments, invokes Wasm, and sends the result back over HTTP.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>05</span><div><strong>Linear memory is not shared Rust memory.</strong><p>Strings cross through the component model’s canonical ABI. Current Dekopon adds JSON encode/decode on both sides.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>06</span><div><strong>Async I/O is not CPU time-slicing.</strong><p>A future host must suspend waiting host calls and also make long-running guest compute yield, or a Tokio worker can still be monopolized.</p></div></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

